          "outcome": "genuine_dismissed",
          "reasons": [],
          "verdict": "false_positive"
        },
        {
          "case_id": "BenchmarkTest00574",
          "expected": "true_positive",
          "explanation": "The ternary expression `(7 * 42) - num > 200` evaluates to `(294 - 106) > 200` (i.e. `188 > 200`), which is always false, causing `bar` to be assigned `param`. However, `argsEnv` is passed as the environment parameter (`envp`) to `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd, argsEnv)`, not as command-line arguments, and `bar` does not define `FOO=...` needed by the script. Even if it reached environment settings, `Runtime.exec(String, String[])` treats elements as `name=value` pairs, making arbitrary command injection in `cmd` impossible without a shell parser running `cmd`.",
          "outcome": "genuine_dismissed",
          "reasons": [],
          "verdict": "false_positive"
        },
        {
          "case_id": "BenchmarkTest01056",
          "expected": "true_positive",
          "explanation": "The condition `(7 * 42) - num > 200` evaluates at compile/runtime to `294 - 106 > 200`, which is `188 > 200` (evaluating to false). As a result, `bar` is set to `param`, which holds the decoded Referer header, but looking closely: wait, `(7 * 42) - 106 = 294 - 106 = 188`. 188 > 200 is false, so it chooses the false branch (`param`). Thus `bar` is `param`, which is directly written to the HTTP response via `response.getWriter().write` without encoding or sanitization, causing reflected XSS.",
          "outcome": "genuine_dismissed",
          "reasons": [],
